    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Previous field, laboratory and numerical investigations have focused on the\r\ndevelopment of, and appropriate initial and boundary conditions for, a physically-based fracture network simulator(PBNS). In this work we demonstrate that the PBNS can accurately predict the evolution of experimentally generated fracture sets. Analyses of the simulated networks suggest that the flow characteristics of pseudo-symmetric networks are independent of the flaw density and velocity exponent a when a <1. Velocity exponents greater than 1 result in extensive connected pathways at significantly lower fracture densities. Appropriate values of a for natural fracture networks are uncertain.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
